# Line endings: store text as LF and check it out as LF on every OS. Without
# this, a Windows checkout under Git for Windows' default core.autocrlf=true
# writes CRLF working trees, and a per-line parser anchored on `$` stops
# matching (the fleet's EOL standard, hub Story 5.7.1; hub #4452 measured the
# failure in this repo). A clone made before this file landed keeps its CRLF
# working tree until it is repaired per KB gitattributes-eol-pin-stale-checkout:
# delete the files, then `git checkout-index -f -u`. Never
# `git add --renormalize` for it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The repo's own text: LF always, and a textual diff even if a stray control
# byte would make git guess binary.
*.md text eol=lf diff
*.mjs text eol=lf diff
*.json text eol=lf diff
*.tsv text eol=lf diff
*.yml text eol=lf
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf

# Binary files: never converted.
*.jpg binary
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.gz binary

# The originals still on main: git never rewrites their bytes. `license` and
# `_assets/` are 2018 files unchanged since the tag `activewave-2018`.
# `readme.md` and `contributing.md` were rewritten by Story 1.1.2 (#7) and
# follow the *.md rule; their stored bytes were LF already, so the rule
# changes none of them. The rest of the 2018 tree is read at the tag, which
# this file cannot reach: `git show` applies no filter, and `git archive` of
# the tag reads the tag's own attributes, of which it has none. The tag's
# folder names (`software/`, `api/` and the rest) are not listed on purpose:
# the rebuild may reuse them, which is how the retired sparse view hid the
# rebuild's own folders.
license -text
_assets/** -text
